course: dictionary
lesson: 1548
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «The puzzle was challenging, so I had to think hard.» Here challenging means hard, needing effort and skill. The clues are puzzle and think hard.
explain: Sometimes people use a softer word for something bad. «Challenging trading conditions» in business news means business is bad and losing money. Ask yourself: is the writer saying something is hard, or that something is bad? The sentence tells you which.
explain: The word can also describe a look. «She gave me a challenging look» means she was questioning me, as if to say, "Are you sure?" Check each meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the right one.
fact: Challenging means hard, needing effort or skill, as in «a challenging puzzle».
fact: Challenging can also mean bad or very difficult: «a challenging class» means a badly behaved class.
fact: Teachers say «challenging behaviour» instead of bad behaviour, to sound calm and in control.
fact: A challenging look is a questioning look, as if someone is standing up to you.
fact: In a thinking skill called «challenging unhelpful thoughts», you check whether an exaggerated thought is really true.
exercise: «The puzzle was challenging, so Ruby needed hours to solve it.» What does challenging mean here? | Bad and awful | Quick and easy | Questioning | *Hard, needing effort and skill
exercise: The head teacher wrote «challenging behaviour» in the report. What does challenging suggest? | Very clever | Kind and gentle | Hard to see | *Badly behaved, troublesome
exercise: «She gave me a challenging look.» What does it mean? | *She was questioning me | She was lost in thought | She was smiling at me | She was fast asleep
test: «Challenging trading conditions» means business is what? | Good and growing | *Bad, losing money | Hard to count | Quiet and calm
test: Why do adults say challenging instead of bad? | To show they are tired | Because it means exactly the same as nice | *To sound positive and in control | To make the word longer
exam: Which sentence uses challenging to mean bad? | «The climb was challenging, so we stopped twice.» | «The puzzle was challenging, so I took a break.» | «The reading book was challenging for me.» | *«The class was challenging, and the teacher had to sit with them.»
